What:           /proc/gpufreqv2/limit_table (boost command)
Date:           October 2026
Contact:        Tetris device maintainers
Description:
                On single-domain GPUFREQ v2 devices, writing "boost <kHz>"
                requests a UI frequency floor through the existing APIBOOST
                limiter. The lowest frequency at least as high as the request
                is selected from the live GPU working OPP table. The command
                never creates an OPP or changes voltage, limiter priorities,
                enable switches, ceilings, or fixed-frequency controls.

                The number is unsigned decimal. Invalid, unordered, empty or
                oversized tables, a request above the highest supported OPP,
                malformed commands, embedded NULs and writes of 64 bytes or
                more are rejected. Dual-domain devices reject the command.
                "boost 0" releases the UI request without requiring a valid
                working table. Existing set/switch commands remain available.

                UI and legacy GED APIBOOST requests are combined, retaining
                the stronger floor. Releasing UI restores the latest legacy
                request, including changes made while UI was active. The
                APIBOOST ceiling is preserved. A configured DEBUG minimum or
                maximum suspends UI, even when DEBUG switches are disabled.
                UI is removed before a DEBUG write and restored after the
                manual range is cleared. This avoids adding a stronger floor
                under DEBUG's higher priority. Other limiter arbitration and
                the driver's DVFS state handling remain in effect.

                The command has no kernel timeout. PowerHAL must release it
                when the last participating hint ends and reset it on service
                initialization. Requested state is retained if a transport or
                commit fails, so a retry or later limiter request can reconcile
                it. If restoring the legacy floor fails, a DEBUG write is not
                attempted. A failed DEBUG write leaves UI suspended until a
                later successful request. If UI reapplication fails after a
                successful DEBUG clear, the command returns the failure even
                though the DEBUG change has already taken effect.

                gpufreq_status and the existing limiter table report effective
                driver state; a requested floor does not promise a clock while
                the GPU is idle, fixed by another control, or capped by another
                limiter. GPUEB firmware handling requires device validation.
